Specimens in the `Ichthyology` catalog format show the fish measurement form.
Weight units, sex, and life stage work the same way across taxon groups; see
[Measurements](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/).

{% aside type="caution" %}

Ichthyology support is in beta. Check exports against your collection's
requirements before relying on them.

{% /aside %}

## Specimen type

Every fish record starts with `Specimen type`:

- `Individual`: one fish cataloged and stored on its own, with its own
  measurements.
- `Lot`: multiple individuals sharing one catalog number and container, such as
  a jar of fish of the same species from one collecting event.

A record keeps the stored values for both types if you switch, but only the
selected type's fields are shown and exported.

## Individual measurements

- `Sex`: select from the configured specimen-sex controlled vocabulary.
- `Life stage`: select from the configured life-stage controlled vocabulary.
- `Standard length (mm)`: length from the tip of the snout to the end of the
  vertebral column, excluding the caudal fin.
- `Total length (mm)`: length from the tip of the snout to the tip of the
  longest caudal-fin ray.
- `Weight`: specimen weight in the selected unit.
- `Remarks`: additional notes about the specimen.

## Lot measurements

- `Total individuals`: number of fish in the lot, measured or not.
- `Life stage`: life stage of the lot.
- `Auto-update min and max`: when on, NAHPU fills the min/max range fields below
  from the individuals measured in the lot and makes those range fields
  read-only (and raises `Total individuals` if more individuals are entered than
  the recorded count). Turn it off to enter ranges manually, such as when only
  the smallest and largest fish were measured.
- `Min. standard length (mm)` and `Max. standard length (mm)`.
- `Min. total length (mm)` and `Max. total length (mm)`.
- `Min. weight` and `Max. weight`: weight range in the selected unit. Changing
  the lot's weight unit also updates the weight unit stored on its individuals.
- `Remarks`: additional notes about the lot.

`Lot summary` displays the mean, standard deviation (`SD`, when at least two
individuals are measured), count measured (`n`), and range for `Standard length`,
`Total length`, and `Weight`, along with how many of `Total individuals` have
been measured.

## Individuals in a lot

Use `Individuals` on a lot record to log fish measured one by one.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Add individual`.
2. Under `Identity`, enter the `Tag ID` or keep the prefilled tag, then choose
   `Sex` and `Life stage`.
3. Under `Measurements`, enter `SL (mm)`, `TL (mm)`, and `Weight` in the lot's
   weight unit. Add `Remarks` if needed.
4. Select `Save and add another` to enter the next fish, or `Save` to close the
   form.

{% /steps %}

Select any individual in the list to edit it. Deleting an individual removes it
and unlinks it from any specimen parts and symbionts.

## Tag ID numbering

Individual tag IDs can be numbered automatically. Configure the format in
`Settings` > `Specimens` > `Individual tag ID` (shown only when `Ichthyology` is
active), or open the options menu on `Tag ID` and select `Settings`.

| Field | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `Prefix` | Text before the number, such as `FT-` |
| `Next number` | Number assigned to the next individual; leading zeros are preserved (`0007` → `0008`) |
| `Suffix` | Text after the number, such as `-A` |
| `Next tag` | Live preview of the next tag ID |

An auto-numbered tag shows `Auto-numbered. Edit to override.` Typing a different
tag keeps your value without advancing the counter. Select `New number` in the
field menu to restore the next auto-numbered tag. When auto-numbering is not
configured, NAHPU suggests sequential numbers (`1`, `2`, `3`, …) within the lot.

## Parts and symbionts

Fish catalogs include the `Symbionts` examination fields at the bottom of the
measurement form and the symbiont tab in `Specimen Parts`. When a lot has
recorded individuals, specimen parts and symbionts show an `Individuals`
selector: use `Add individual` to link the part or symbiont to the fish it came
from (such as a fin clip from a tagged fish). See
[Specimen Parts](/en/usages/specimens/parts/) and
[Symbionts](/en/usages/specimens/symbionts/).

## Water conditions

Record water conditions on the collecting event rather than on the specimen.
Collecting-event [environmental data](/en/usages/events/) include `Water
temperature`, `pH`, `Dissolved oxygen`, `Dissolved oxygen saturation` (%),
`Conductivity` (µS/cm), and `Flow velocity`.

## Export

Specimen exports for an `Ichthyology` catalog add these measurement columns:
`specimenType`, `individualCount`, `sex`, `lifeStage`, `standardLength`,
`totalLength`, `weight`, `weightUnit`, `minStandardLength`,
`maxStandardLength`, `minTotalLength`, `maxTotalLength`, `minWeight`,
`maxWeight`, and `remark`.

The `individuals` column lists a lot's individuals separated by `|`, formatted
as `ID: FT-0001;SL: 52 mm;TL: 61 mm;Weight: 3.1 g;Sex: Female;Life stage: Adult`
(with `Remark: ...` appended when present). Specimen part and symbiont exports
list their linked individual IDs separated by commas.

In Darwin Core exports, `individualCount` is the lot's `Total individuals`, or
`1` for an individual fish. See
[Export Records](/en/usages/export/export-records/).
